<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaupang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–<a href="/years/960">960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Skiringssal, Sciringes heal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaupang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> trading town near Larvik in Vestfold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, occupied from about 800 to the mid-10th century. It is usually identified with Sciringes heal, the port described by the Norwegian traveller Ohthere to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred of Wessex</a>. Excavations have found workshops, houses and imported goods from Frankish, Danish, British and Arab lands, making it Norway's earliest known town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaupang.jpg" alt="Kaupang" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nortonius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skiringssal_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kaupang lay on a beach by the Viksfjorden, a branch of the Larviksfjorden, near the present-day village of Tjøllingvollen in Larvik Municipality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> hall of Skiringssal stood just inland from the fjord, and written sources indicate that the area was an important royal seat in the 8th and 9th centuries. The name is Old Norse for a market-place, from kaup-, to buy, and angr, a fjord harbour, so that it means something like buy harbour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence suggests that Kaupang may have been the first proto-urban settlement of any significance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Excavation has shown a centre of crafts and commerce with around 1,000 inhabitants, varied craft production and extensive trade with foreign lands, dealing in iron, soapstone and perhaps fish. Why the town was abandoned is unknown.</p><p>The first excavations were made in 1867 by Nicolay Nicolaysen, who mapped one of the grave-fields around the settlement, dug 79 grave mounds and found a cremation cemetery dated largely to the 10th century. Charlotte Blindheim began excavating in 1947 and published her last work in 1999. From 2000 to 2002 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>'s Institute for Archaeology, Conservation and Historical Studies dug at the site under Dagfinn Skre, followed by a smaller excavation of the harbour area in 2003. Four possible houses were found, with hearths, pits and postholes, and the results appeared in three books between 2007 and 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kaupang has been described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s most important monument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. Many of the approximately 100,000 finds from its excavations, among them Arab silver coins, a gold coin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorestad">Dorestad</a>, hundreds of glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, gold and bronze jewellery, pottery, weapons and tools, have been displayed at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaupang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Market towns, Ribe, Hedeby at the base of Jutland, Birka on Lake Mälaren and <b>Kaupang</b> in Norway, drew craftsmen who made combs, beads, jewellery and weapons, and merchants who dealt in furs, walrus ivory, amber, slaves and silver.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In trading towns such as Hedeby, Birka and <b>Kaupang</b> lived craftsmen and merchants, and free but landless servants (húskarlar) worked on other men’s farms.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings controlled <b>Kaupang</b>, the trading town the traveller Ohthere called Sciringes heal, where goods from the Frankish, Danish and Arab worlds were exchanged.</blockquote><a href="/state/vestfold">Vestfold</a></li></ul></section>
